The attached image, Fig. 2, visually characterizes the physical properties of Nano-11 nanoparticles, which are being investigated as vaccine adjuvants. Panel A shows a Transmission Electron Microscopy image revealing spherical particles with an irregular, cauliflower-like appearance, with a 50 nm scale bar. Panel B illustrates the hydrodynamic diameter of Nano-11, showing a relatively uniform size distribution with a Z-average diameter between 75 and 80 nm. Panel C presents the zeta potential of Nano-11 across various pH levels, indicating a positive surface charge ranging from +7 to +15 mV, which contributes to its convenient dispersion in aqueous systems. Panel D demonstrates Nano-11's ability to adsorb negatively charged ovalbumin (OVA) but not positively charged lysozyme, suggesting that antigen binding is primarily mediated by electrostatic interactions.